microtun-firmware-signer for Debian
====================================

The package intentionally installs the systemd unit disabled and does not start
it during installation. The installed /etc/microtun-firmware-signer/config.toml
is an example configuration and must be reviewed before first use.

The package creates the dedicated microtun-firmware-signer system user and group
during configuration. If an encrypted signing key is stored under
/etc/microtun-firmware-signer, a typical
secure ownership/mode is:

  root:microtun-firmware-signer 0640

The application also enforces that encrypted key files are not group-writable
and are not accessible by other users.

Signing-key passphrases are intentionally not systemd credentials. Every active
key starts locked after service startup. The packaged unit creates
/run/microtun-firmware-signer and the service exposes its local admin API
at /run/microtun-firmware-signer/admin.sock. The socket defaults to mode 0660.
Operators who need to unlock keys should be granted access to the service group,
or invoke the unlock subcommand as an appropriately privileged user:

  microtun-firmware-signer unlock --key microtun-firmware-prod

The unlock subcommand reads the passphrase directly from the terminal with hidden
input in Rust and never accepts the key passphrase as a command-line argument or
environment variable.

The GitHub OAuth client secret remains a systemd encrypted credential. The
credential name matches GitHub.OAuthClientSecretCredential in the example
configuration:

  LoadCredentialEncrypted=github-oauth-client-secret:/etc/credstore.encrypted/microtun-github-oauth-client-secret

Deployment-specific credential mappings should normally be placed in a systemd
drop-in instead of editing the packaged unit, for example:

  /etc/systemd/system/microtun-firmware-signer.service.d/credentials.conf

After configuring the TOML, signing key file, and OAuth systemd credential:

  systemctl daemon-reload
  systemctl enable --now microtun-firmware-signer.service
  microtun-firmware-signer unlock --key microtun-firmware-prod
  systemctl status microtun-firmware-signer.service

The package installs /usr/bin/microtun-firmware-signer. The same binary provides
the `unlock` subcommand.
